    <details class="help-item" id="collection-numbers">
        <summary>What is a collection number?</summary>
        <p>
            The short number some people write on each pot, like 117. It is optional. Set it in the
            <em>Collection number</em> field on the plant's edit form, where the next free number is one tap away.
            A cutting from plant 117 gets 117a, then 117b, so the label on the jar says where it came from.
            When a cutting is potted up, the plant keeps its number. If several are potted up together, they get
            117c1, 117c2 and so on. To number the plants you already have, oldest first, open
            <em>Collection numbers</em> in Settings. Two plants with the same number are pointed out, but
            Stikling never changes a number for you.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item" id="labels">
        <summary>How do I print labels?</summary>
        <p>
            Pick the plants or cuttings first. On the Plants or Propagations page, tap <em>Select</em>, tick them and tap
            <em>Labels</em>. For one plant or cutting, open <em>More</em> on its page and choose <em>Print a label</em>. After you
            start a propagation or pot one up, the page offers a label too. Each label has the collection number, large, with the
            name beside it. You can add the botanical name and the date, and remove a label with the cross on its corner without
            touching the plant.
        </p>
        <p>
            There are three ways to get them out. <em>Label maker app</em> is for a phone label maker like a Niimbot or a Phomemo:
            pick its label size, and the labels go to the share menu as pictures, where you choose the maker's app. A browser
            that can't share pictures, like Firefox on Android, saves them instead, and you open them in the app.
            <em>Tape printer</em> is for a tape printer like a Brother P-touch or a Dymo that is installed on a computer. Pick the
            tape and the length, tap Print and choose the printer in the print dialog. <em>Sheet</em> is for a normal printer,
            on A4 or Letter paper, with small tags for pots or long strips for plant stakes, both with cut lines.
        </p>
        <p>
            The size and what to show are remembered on this device. Nothing is saved on the plants themselves. On tape 12 mm wide
            or less there is room for the number and the name only.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>@Strings.Help.NameUnderPlantSBotanicalQ</summary>
        <p>@((MarkupString)Strings.Help.NameUnderPlantSBotanicalA1)</p>
    </details>

